---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const chartsCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Pick the encoding by question', note: 'Comparison → bars. Trend over time → line. Relationship → scatter. Part-of-whole → stacked bar (pie charts fail beyond ~3 slices — humans compare angles badly).' },
    { math: '2 · Zero-base your bars', note: 'Bar length encodes value; a bar chart truncated at 90% turns a 2% change into a visual cliff. Line charts may truncate (position, not length, encodes) — but must say so.' },
    { math: '3 · Order and colour are information', note: 'Sort bars by value, not alphabetically. Use one accent colour for the thing you\'re discussing; grey everything else. Rainbow palettes create fake boundaries (Cairo\'s "chartjunk" rule).' },
    { math: '4 · Title states the finding', note: '"Sales by region" makes the reader work. "Region C drives the decline" states the takeaway — a chart title is a conclusion, not a caption. Then show the uncertainty if there is any.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/data-science/intermediate/charts-that-tell-the-truth">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Visualisation is <strong>argument by encoding</strong>: whatever you map to size, colour and
    position becomes the claim the audience absorbs instantly — before reading a word. Honest
    charts align the encoding with the data's importance; dishonest ones (truncated axes,
    cherry-picked windows, dual axes) mislead without a single false word.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four rules of charts that tell the truth" widget="equationStep" config={chartsCfg} />

  <h2>The classics of graphical honesty</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Dual axes are a trap:</strong> two unrelated series on two scales can "correlate" perfectly. If you must, mark the scales loudly and test the claim without the second axis.</li>
    <li><strong>Perception research is the foundation:</strong> Cleveland & McGill showed position and length are judged accurately; area, saturation and hue are judged poorly. Encode your most important variable with position.</li>
    <li><strong>Annotate the story:</strong> mark the product launch, the policy change, the outlier's name. Charts with annotations explain; charts without them require explanation.</li>
    <li><strong>Show the uncertainty:</strong> error bars, ranges or "based on n = 40" notes. A confident line without an interval is a claim disguised as a chart.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Tufte, The Visual Display of Quantitative Information; Cleveland & McGill, Science (1984); Cairo, The Truthful Art.</p>
</TopicPage>
